  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is near zero (0.00) and recent history shows a downtrend or consolidation, the model systematically underestimates volatility by predicting a smooth upward drift; true outcomes often exhibit sharp spikes followed by mean reversion rather than linear growth.",
    "The model exhibits a significant lag in reacting to high-impact price spikes found in the last 3-5 days of history (e.g., Example 5's jump from 68.02 to 71.56); forecasts fail to extrapolate these momentum events, resulting in consistent under-prediction during volatile periods.",
    "In low-sentiment environments, the model assumes a steady mean-reversion trajectory that ignores the 'fat tail' risk of sudden reversals; true outcomes frequently deviate by 3-5% from the forecasted path within the first 10 days.",
    "When history shows a recent peak (e.g., Example 3's 69.99), the model incorrectly projects continued linear growth, whereas true outcomes tend to oscillate around the previous high or drop sharply before recovering."
  ]
